+# Agent Note: macOS identity point queries
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-15-macos-identity-point-query.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The macOS inspector reads the whole process table to check one retained identity before signalling. Cleanup repeats that cost for each target even though it already has a tree snapshot.
+
+## Decision
+
+Current identity checks select one PID with `ps -p` and retain the existing start-timestamp comparison. Tree snapshots still enumerate the full table. A headerless selected-PID query returning status `1` with empty stdout and stderr means absence; all other query errors propagate.
+
+The [persistent PTY](../feature/2026-07-16-persistent-pty-sessions.md) and [native containment](../architecture/2026-08-28-subprocess-native-containment.md) decisions remain active: this query change does not alter readiness, managed-range ownership, or fallback cleanup guarantees.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Reuse the snapshot before signalling.** Rejected because an earlier observation cannot detect PID reuse between discovery and signal delivery.
+
+**Treat every query failure as absence.** Rejected because an unreadable process table does not prove that the managed range is empty.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Each fresh macOS identity check avoids full-table enumeration without adding a cache, configuration, or lifecycle owner. Tree discovery costs and the existing timestamp precision remain unchanged. Focused tests cover current identities, changed start timestamps, absent PIDs, query failures, and full-table snapshots.
